import{_ as t,d as a,e as n,x as i}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Part 10 Multi-Circle Objects and Joint States","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 10 Multi-Circle Objects and Joint States","xkChars":111,"xkWords":4283},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-10.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-10.md"}'),s={name:"en/theory/part-10.md"};function o(r,e,l,c,d,p){return a(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="theory-source"><p><a id="part_10"></a></p><h1 id="part-title-10" tabindex="-1">Part 10 Multi-Circle Objects and Joint States <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-title-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Part 10 Multi-Circle Objects and Joint States {#part-title-10}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Analyzing a single object or circle<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> often compresses other conditions into a black box called “environment.” Multi-circle analysis brings into one joint state the structural units that can change the path of an event and that can be distinguished by boundaries, relations, channels, and time windows. The aim does not require adding background without limit. Families, teams, institutions, platforms, markets, cultural communities, bodily systems, and geographic spaces can all matter in the same event. Naming them does not automatically supply grounds for object facts about them or support for a grouping gain.</p><h2 id="section-10-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Candidate Object Contract<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">[2]</a></sup> for a Circle <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.1 The Candidate Object Contract[^tn-contract-11] for a Circle {#section-10-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A circle can be represented as follows.</p><p><code>C* = &lt;圈层 ID，候选边界，成员资格，角色规则，进入与退出，内部状态，外部接口，物质通道，体验—意义通道，约束，时间窗，尺度剖面，同一性判据，证据状态，反例&gt;</code>.<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn3" id="fnref3">[3]</a></sup></p><p>“Several similar people” does not suffice to define a circle. A circle need not have shared consciousness or a unified goal. Members may not know that the analyst has grouped them into the same circle. They may disagree about goals, and a candidate structure may form solely through shared exposure to a platform, rule, space, or risk. Documents, memberships, sustained interactions, or shared exposure can support the corresponding facts about the object. A further claim that this grouping improves explanation or foresight requires testing for a gain within the stated scope against a complexity-matched alternative grouping or no-grouping baseline. Support under G1 concerns eligibility for this latter task-specific claim. It does not decide whether the organization, relationships, or people affected exist.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Admission Question</th><th>Evidence Required</th><th>If the Check Does Not Pass</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Can the boundary be distinguished?</td><td>Membership rules, interfaces, shared exposure, or sustained interaction</td><td>Retain it as the analyst’s grouping and do not call it an objective circle</td></tr><tr><td>Is there differentiated structure within it?</td><td>Roles, relations, channels, or constraints</td><td>Reclassify it as a population category or an environmental variable</td></tr><tr><td>If a grouping gain is claimed, does the grouping improve explanation or foresight?</td><td>Out-of-sample comparison with a simple baseline</td><td>Do not claim that a gain has been established. Retain facts about the object that have independent grounds. Depending on the task, fall back to a single focus, continuous variables, or a candidate grouping</td></tr><tr><td>Does identity hold within the time window?</td><td>K, entry and exit, and conditions for conversion and dissolution</td><td>Record separate segments or register object conversion</td></tr><tr><td>Have counterexamples been retained?</td><td>Boundary perturbation or review across time or locations</td><td>Report the sensitivity and narrow the related conclusions; a grouping gain already claimed is handled under §4.3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="section-10-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Joint Object Contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.2 The Joint Object Contract {#section-10-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The multi-circle joint object is written as follows.</p><p><code>MC* = &lt;行动者集合 A，圈层集合 C，圈层关系 Rcc，成员与角色映射 Rac，物质状态 M，体验—意义状态 Ψ，通道与约束 Q，事件流 E，多时钟 T，尺度剖面 SP，证据状态 W，同一性判据 K&gt;</code>.<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn4" id="fnref4">[4]</a></sup></p><p>The value of a joint object lies in preserving local differences and cross-boundary channels. It does not put all related objects into one large circle or assert that a higher-level subject exists. Circles with no identifiable relation may only be observed side by side. If they share only an environment, a direct effect should not be invented. If the relation appears only during an event, register it as temporary and state the conditions under which it ends.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Joint State Field</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Common Error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A Actor set</td><td>Preserve actor states, roles, and privacy boundaries</td><td>Substituting a circle average for the individual</td></tr><tr><td>C Circle set</td><td>Maintain boundaries, states, and identity separately</td><td>Merging all background conditions into one overarching circle</td></tr><tr><td>Rcc Circle relations</td><td>Register direction, type, delay, threshold, and evidence</td><td>Substituting “interrelated” for a mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>Rac Membership and role mapping</td><td>Allow multiple affiliations, role conflicts, and differences in ability to exit</td><td>Assuming each person belongs to only one circle</td></tr><tr><td>M and Ψ</td><td>Represent material and experience–meaning conditions side by side</td><td>Material determinism or reduction to narrative alone</td></tr><tr><td>Q Channels and constraints</td><td>Specify the extent and capacity of propagation</td><td>Assuming impacts are lossless, instantaneous, and reach every position</td></tr><tr><td>E and T</td><td>Event stream and multiple asynchronous clocks</td><td>Updating every state synchronously after each event</td></tr><tr><td>W and K</td><td>Evidence, unknowns, residuals, and object identity</td><td>Mistaking a complete model for a complete account of reality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="section-10-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Six Kinds of Circle Relation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3 Six Kinds of Circle Relation {#section-10-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The six starting points for description distinguish different questions. They are nonexclusive, address different dimensions, and do not exhaust real-world relations. Parallelism, nesting, and overlap describe positions along a declared dimension. Bridging and competition describe connections or constraints. Temporariness describes duration and conditions for ending. These descriptions can be combined. For each actual circle relation, separately record the source circle, target circle, direction, shared members or interfaces, channel, extent, time window, and relevant evidence. Do not force a value for an inapplicable delay or threshold.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Parallelism <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.1 Parallelism {#section-10-3-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Under the current contract, parallel circles have no relation of containment in either membership or the scope of their operating contracts. The circles may still be related. Two parallel teams may share a budget, a platform, and higher-level institutional arrangements. They may also be triggered simultaneously by the same event. If only shared exposure can be confirmed, register a shared environment. Register cross-circle propagation only when a channel for information, resources, personnel, or institutional write-back has been found.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Nesting <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.2 Nesting {#section-10-3-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Nesting means that one object’s extent is actually contained within another along an explicitly specified relational dimension. The basis of containment must be stated. Membership, roles, resource accounting, institutional jurisdiction, and space are distinct dimensions. If the claim concerns containment of only a part, first identify the contained subobject and its boundary. If two sets intersect but neither contains the other, register overlap on that dimension. Describing the intersection as “partial containment” does not also justify classifying it as nesting. A subcircle’s state cannot directly represent the higher-level circle, and the outer circle’s rules need not take effect in the same way at every position inside it.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Overlap <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.3 Overlap {#section-10-3-3}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Overlap means that circles share some members, roles, resources, or interfaces without fully containing one another. Positions of overlap may take on bridging, translation, and coordination. They may also face role conflict, conflicting loyalties, duplicated work, and risks of information leakage. The proportion of overlap cannot substitute for a record of what is actually shared.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Bridging <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.4 Bridging {#section-10-3-4}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Bridging means that an actor, institution, interface, text, platform, or event connects previously separate circles. A bridging party need not have authority to represent others or be loyal to either side. Transmission capability, translation losses, selective filtering, responsibility, substitutability, overload, and exit conditions must be registered separately.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Competition <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.5 Competition {#section-10-3-5}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Competition means that circles create identifiable exclusionary constraints on scarce resources, attention, legitimacy, member commitments, interpretive authority, or room for action. Different goals do not automatically amount to competition. Register competition only when one circle’s use of what is available, its rules, or its actions changes the set available to another circle. Cooperation and competition can coexist through different channels.</p><h3 id="section-10-3-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.3.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Temporariness <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-3-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3.6 Temporariness {#section-10-3-6}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Temporary circles form around events, tasks, crises, shared exposure, or short-term goals. They may dissolve rapidly or become more durable through traces, stabilization of roles, and institutionalization. Temporariness may follow from a task deadline set in advance or be a provisional judgment based on the short window observed so far. The two should be distinguished. A temporary relation can be important, carry responsibilities, and already be real. A temporary alliance can still overlap in membership, compete for resources, and be bridged through particular interfaces.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>Minimum Criterion</th><th>What to Check in Particular</th><th>Failure or Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parallelism</td><td>No containment relation at present</td><td>Shared environment and indirect coupling</td><td>Containment, overlap, or bridging emerges</td></tr><tr><td>Nesting</td><td>Explicit containment in membership/contract/jurisdiction/space</td><td>Downward constraints and loss from upward aggregation</td><td>K changes, exit occurs, or jurisdiction is reconfigured</td></tr><tr><td>Overlap</td><td>Some members, roles, resources, or interfaces are shared</td><td>Role conflict and double counting</td><td>Shared items disappear or the relation becomes containment</td></tr><tr><td>Bridging</td><td>Identifiable interface for cross-circle transmission</td><td>Translation, filtering, overload, and authority to represent</td><td>The channel closes, is replaced, or becomes institutionalized</td></tr><tr><td>Competition</td><td>An exclusionary constraint on the available set</td><td>Scarce items, time window, and channels for cooperation</td><td>Resources expand, rules are coordinated, or a party exits</td></tr><tr><td>Temporariness</td><td>A clearly finite duration or support so far limited to a short window. Distinguish the two</td><td>Ending, traces, and who takes up responsibility. Temporariness can be combined with other dimensions of relation</td><td>The temporary relation ends, is extended, or becomes institutionalized. Its positions and channel relations are recorded separately</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>“Relation change” records a shift in a relation’s type or defining conditions between the states before and after an event. Referring back to the relation by the same name does not by itself establish that it has changed. Relations can persist, strengthen, weaken, change direction, change type, or dissolve. Every judgment must be tied to snapshots before and after, the dimension that changed, and object identity.</p><h2 id="section-10-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Relations Do Not Form a Single Tree <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.4 Relations Do Not Form a Single Tree {#section-10-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Actual multi-circle structures typically form a directed multigraph of relations with local containment. The same family can be nested within a community and within a legal system. A person can belong at the same time to overlapping family, work, platform, and interest circles. Platforms may bridge parallel groups. Different organizations can compete for funding, cooperate on safety, and overlap in personnel.</p><p>The framework does not require each node to have only one parent node. Nor does “higher level” automatically mean greater importance, reality, or authority. Each edge expresses just one declared relation. If a pair of circles has multiple edges, record the channel and time window for each edge separately. If edges at different scales are incomparable, keep them side by side without forcing an order.</p><p>The graph representation must pass at least four checks. Has the analyst’s classification been mistaken for an object? Has a shared environment been mistaken for a direct relation? Has membership overlap been mistaken for organizational containment? Has contact with information been mistaken for effective feedback? Register feedback only when a signal returns and changes a subsequent state, transition probability, or constraint.</p><h2 id="section-10-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Multiple Memberships and Role Conflicts <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.5 Multiple Memberships and Role Conflicts {#section-10-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Membership cannot be reduced to a binary, permanent status. Records should include the basis for membership, start and end times, roles, strength of commitment, actual participation, ability to exit, and disputes. Formal members may lack the capability to act. Informal participants may bear key costs. People drawn in involuntarily may be unable to leave. Representatives may lack authorization from the people they represent.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Membership Dimension</th><th>Example States</th><th>Implication for Projection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Basis for entry</td><td>Voluntary, employment, kinship, law, platform rules, shared exposure, or coercion</td><td>Determines the limits of responsibility and exit</td></tr><tr><td>Extent of participation</td><td>Core, intermittent, peripheral, nominal, or passively bearing the consequences</td><td>Determines visible information and channels of impact</td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>Decision-making, execution, care, bridging, oversight, affected party, or dissent</td><td>Determines rights, obligations, and conflicts</td></tr><tr><td>Commitment</td><td>High, medium, low, disputed, or unknown</td><td>Affects paths without amounting to personality</td></tr><tr><td>Ability to exit</td><td>Free, costly, subject to approval, or impossible in practice</td><td>Triggers the protection floor and the action ceiling</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An actor’s multiple memberships do not automatically establish actual bridging. Potential bridging capability, actual transmission across boundaries, and outcomes caused by transmission are recorded separately. An identifiable interface supports a description of capability. Records of actual transmission support a description of the process. A causal effect on state requires a corresponding comparison. If recipients do not change their choices, this does not negate the limited transmission of information that already occurred. Authority to represent, transmission capability, and responsible parties must be kept distinct.</p><h2 id="section-10-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Material Conditions and Experience–Meaning Conditions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.6 Material Conditions and Experience–Meaning Conditions {#section-10-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The joint state maintains both M and Ψ. M includes the body, time, space, money, energy, equipment, infrastructure, property rights, supply, care, and actual capability to act. Ψ includes feelings, expectations, memory, identity, trust, shame, hope, fear, commitments, conflicts of value, and perceived legitimacy.</p><p>The two channels can affect one another. For example, a platform’s rules for traffic change visibility and income, affecting material and informational conditions. Members interpret the decline as exclusion or failure, a change in experience and meaning. This interpretation may affect exit, protest, or cooperation and thereby feed back into platform metrics and resource allocation. Each step must specify a carrier, direction, delay, and counterexamples.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Bridge Between Channels</th><th>Starting Point</th><th>Mediating Factors</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Counterfactual Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>M→Ψ</td><td>A change in income, space, the body, or time</td><td>Sense of safety, sense of control, or interpretation of identity</td><td>Changes in trust, fear, or commitment</td><td>Do interpretations differ under the same material change?</td></tr><tr><td>Ψ→M</td><td>Legitimacy, hope, shame, or conflicts of value</td><td>Cooperation, refusal, migration, care, or protest</td><td>Changes in resource flows, labor, property rights, or use of facilities</td><td>Would the action occur without the change in meaning?</td></tr><tr><td>M↔Ψ Closed loop</td><td>Effects in both directions occur in succession</td><td>Feedback and traces</td><td>Path dependence or phase transition</td><td>Does the return signal actually change a subsequent state?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Analyzing material and experience–meaning conditions side by side requires neither equal weights nor full collection on both sides for every narrow task. If the objective concerns members’ control, well-being, or relationships, relevant differences on both sides should be retained. For a question such as the total amount of equipment, for which an adequate summary is already available, the analysis may stop within that scope.</p><h3 id="section-10-6-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.6.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Resource Conversion and Multiple Positions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-6-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.6.1 Resource Conversion and Multiple Positions {#section-10-6-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Resources create usable opportunities only under specific conditions for conversion. Income pays current expenses. Wealth provides a buffer or collateral. Qualifications and skills need recognition from the relevant institutions. Relationships must be possible to mobilize, and the other party must actually have resources and be willing to help. Transportation, time, care, standing under the law, information, and the cost of refusal may cause equal amounts of resources to yield different practical options. These quantities cannot be added into a single capital or power score without specifying units, conversion rules, double counting, weights, and purpose. A well-grounded monetary valuation or an index for a specific task can answer the question within its limits. A total cannot fill in each person’s control, qualifications, or well-being. A nominal allocation cannot substitute for resources actually at the recipient’s disposal.</p><p>The combined effects of multiple positions need not take the form of simple overlap among several circles. Rules of admission, accumulation of seniority, evaluation, and redress within the same institutional arrangement may change a group’s opportunities in succession. For example, later entry into a position where seniority accrues can continue to produce differences when opportunities are subsequently allocated uniformly by seniority. The sequence of rules, the denominators for entry at each stage, comparison groups, and positions that are absent should be traced. The number of categories, an interaction term, or a group average does not by itself establish the cause of harm to an individual.</p><p>Different statuses must not be put in a fixed order of disadvantage in advance. The same cue may receive different responses in different jobs, locations, relationships, or periods. Retain differences within categories, those who did not enter, and those who succeeded. Adding sensitive classifications is warranted only if it can answer a clear question, the material is sufficient to support it, and the privacy cost is tolerable. Do not compel disclosure to fill a cross-tabulation or treat the positions as nonexistent because the material cannot be obtained.</p><h3 id="section-10-6-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.6.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Assets, Liabilities, Contracts, and Transmission Through Credit <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-6-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.6.2 Assets, Liabilities, Contracts, and Transmission Through Credit {#section-10-6-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In financial relations, the material state must at least distinguish stocks at a point in time from flows over a period, asset valuations from funds that can be converted into cash, principal payable from interest, and net worth from the ability to refinance. Assets in excess of liabilities do not guarantee that cash will be available when payments are due. A new loan does not by itself repair a shortfall in asset value. The market price of collateral, the contractual borrowing limit, the actual outstanding loan balance, and recovery on default are distinct quantities. Each must be tied to the currency, maturity, contract, repayment priority, and observation window.</p><p>One testable path of amplification starts with changes in assets or cash flow that alter net worth and the amount that can be pledged as collateral. Through loan quotes, credit limits, loan extensions, or margin calls, these changes affect net financing. Changes in financing then affect consumption, investment, hiring, and subsequent income and feed back into demand for assets. A candidate for self-reinforcement may be proposed only when the relevant constraints are binding and cash, equity, alternative loans, or arrangements for bearing risk have not cut the chain. Earnings expectations, interest rates, or policy may also drive prices and spending jointly. Changes in the same direction are insufficient to identify a financial accelerator mechanism.</p><p>In a financial network, common exposure, defaults on direct claims, calls on contractual guarantees, price spillovers from fire sales, and withdrawals of funds prompted by information must be recorded separately. Risk transfer changes who holds the risk and what they can bear. Whether it reduces overall risk still depends on the receiving party’s capital, contingent obligations, and who ultimately bears losses. The face values of mutual claims and liabilities across multiple layers must not be added up as net wealth for the system. Resumption of payments, restoration of capital, recovery of borrowers’ cash flow, and recovery of living conditions are different outcomes.</p><h2 id="section-10-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Five Kinds of Clock and Asynchronous Updating <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.7 Five Kinds of Clock and Asynchronous Updating {#section-10-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A joint state distinguishes at least immediate, interactive, organizational, institutional, and long-term clocks. Immediate reactions may be measured in minutes, trust in interaction in rounds, organizational budgets in quarters, institutional write-back in deliberation cycles, and long-term learning or generational change in years. The timing of the same event’s effects varies by variable.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Clock</th><th>Typical Objects</th><th>Update Trigger</th><th>Common Error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Immediate</td><td>Emotion, attention, bodily arousal, local prices</td><td>Arrival of a signal or a direct shock</td><td>Recording short-term fluctuations as long-term change</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive</td><td>Trust, strategies, expectations in relationships, local norms</td><td>Several rounds of action and feedback</td><td>Ignoring the order of rounds and memory</td></tr><tr><td>Organizational</td><td>Roles, budgets, processes, metrics</td><td>Cycles of decision, execution, and review</td><td>Assuming a decision amounts to implementation</td></tr><tr><td>Institutional</td><td>Rules, rights, responsibilities, appeals, records</td><td>Actual change in rules or procedures, actual write-back, and execution. Record authorization and legal validity separately</td><td>Treating a verbal commitment as institutional change</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term</td><td>Capabilities, identity, infrastructure, culture, and relations across generations</td><td>Accumulation, learning, maintenance, and replacement</td><td>Extrapolating to the long term from short-term data</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>External events directly update only variables reached through their channels and for which the conditions hold. Endogenous processes, expiration, accumulation, and recovery proceed on their own clocks and under the registered rules for dynamics. A variable untouched by the current event is not necessarily unchanged in reality. Without reliable grounds for an update, retain the last observed value, its time, and uncertainty, or mark it unknown. Do not refresh all variables at once just to make the joint snapshot “complete.”</p><p>Asynchrony can also change the direction of effects. Asset prices can change immediately, while loan contracts adjust only upon revaluation or renewal. The timing of payments differs between fixed-rate and floating-rate debt. Public guarantees may provide cash first and distribute losses later. A joint projection should check when each constraint actually binds, when alternative financing is possible, and whether the recovery window precedes irreversible harm. Extending a short-term backstop must not be treated as restoration of the long-term ability to repay.</p><h2 id="section-10-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Joint State Snapshots <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.8 Joint State Snapshots {#section-10-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A snapshot is a frozen starting point for projection. It does not permanently reproduce reality. It must include a timestamp, evidence cutoff, actor and circle references, relation and membership mappings, M/Ψ states, channels, constraints, the five kinds of clock, scale profile, unknowns, residuals, and identity criteria.</p><p>Each value in a snapshot should be marked as observed, supported hypothesis, candidate, disputed, unknown, or not applicable. When sources conflict, first retain their respective values, how they were produced, and their versions. A combined estimate and its uncertainty may be reported separately only if there are grounds for both the measured object and the model used to combine results. An arithmetic mean cannot resolve the original conflict by itself. If a circle is internally differentiated, do not represent all positions with a single mean. If necessary, establish subcircles or use a distributional representation, but go through object admission again.</p><h2 id="section-10-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Boundary Perturbation and Object Conversion <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.9 Boundary Perturbation and Object Conversion {#section-10-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Multi-circle models can easily mistake chosen boundaries for natural boundaries. At least three kinds of perturbation must be tested. Change the membership threshold and check whether the conclusion is stable. Move the time window and check whether the relation still holds. Replace or remove a circle and check whether the joint model’s gain disappears. If small changes in the boundary reverse the conclusion, narrow the conclusions that depend on that boundary and report the sensitivity; a grouping gain already claimed is recorded under §4.3 as not supported or unresolved.</p><p>If a circle’s K no longer holds after an event, object conversion or dissolution should be registered. For example, a temporary rescue group may become a formal institution, a project team may split into competing alliances, a platform community may migrate to a new platform, or a household’s care arrangements may be reorganized after a member exits. A new object cannot overwrite the old object’s failures, responsibilities, or historical traces.</p><h2 id="section-10-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Complexity Check for Joint States <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.10 The Complexity Check for Joint States {#section-10-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Adding circles increases the ability to fit the data and also increases error, data requirements, and degrees of freedom in explanation. Each added circle or relation must be assessed on which residual in the original model it explains, whether it has an independently observable channel, and whether it improves out-of-sample explanation or foresight. Its measurement costs and privacy risks must also be specified, and the conclusion checked with that circle or relation removed.</p><p>Multi-circle models must be compared with a simple single-focus or single-circle baseline. If a gain in prediction or explanation is claimed but the complex model only tells a fuller story about the materials already observed, without stable comparative support, downgrade the claim of gain and prefer an adequate simpler model. Keeping an object to describe established responsibilities, differences among members, or lawful permissions need not first establish a predictive gain. The independent question it answers should still be stated, recording costs should be controlled, and more data should not be treated as better explanation. Complexity does not amount to maturity. Using one fewer circle is usually better if no important distinction is lost.</p><h2 id="section-10-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>An Example of a Joint Object <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.11 An Example of a Joint Object {#section-10-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An analysis of an employee’s public departure from a job can identify the actor, project team, company management, family care circle, professional community, and public opinion on a platform at the same time. The team is nested within the company. The employee belongs to the overlapping team, family, and professional circles. The platform bridges the employee and the public outside the company. The team and management may compete over resource allocation but cooperate in maintaining reputation. A group that forms around the departure to express support may be a temporary circle.</p><p>Material conditions include pay, working hours, care, contracts, job positions, and platform traffic. Experience–meaning conditions include dignity, a sense of betrayal, professional identity, trust in coworkers, and perceived legitimacy among the public. The departure immediately changes attention and emotion, then affects trust and handover in the team. Organizational processes may change weeks later, and institutional rules may be written back later still. Any single-track explanation that the person “left because of their personality” or that the departure was “entirely determined by company structure” must be compared with the joint state.</p><p>The snapshot makes different paths visible. If the company responds only through public relations without changing working hours or appeal channels, emotions on the platform may subside while internal distrust persists. If resources are adjusted but repair of meaning fails, people may stay while their commitments continue to weaken. If the pressure of family care is lifted, the actor’s available options change. If the professional community provides resources through a bridge, the cost of exit decreases and others may follow the example. The next Part uses event-driven projection to examine which path the evidence supports.</p><h2 id="section-10-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">10.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Where This Part Stops <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-10-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.12 Where This Part Stops {#section-10-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The layer for multi-circle joint states can identify which circles need to be represented together and whether their relations are parallel, nested, overlapping, bridged, competitive, or temporary. It can also describe how memberships and roles are distributed across circles, how material and experience–meaning conditions are represented side by side, and which clocks are out of sync. It can assess whether the complex model offers a gain relative to a simple baseline.</p><p>A structural graph alone cannot establish what must happen in the future. Nor can it decide which circle most warrants protection, who should bear the costs, or who has authority to act. Event propagation, branching, and cascades belong to Part 11. Conditional foresight and bounded choice belong to Part 12. Normative judgments, authorization, and protection remain the responsibility of later layers.</p><p>If an event involves only one actor and one stable circle, and neighboring circles, membership overlap, bridging, and cross-boundary spillovers provide no additional information, do not use the full joint model. Multi-circle modeling makes it possible to preserve distinctions where interactions actually occur. An analysis need not bear that complexity every time. When fewer objects, fewer variables, and clearer evidence suffice to answer the question, the framework should scale back.</p></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Circles may be parallel, nested, overlapping, or bridged. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn2" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: A contract here is an analytical agreement registered and frozen before results, akin to pre-registration. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn3" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: The full tuple in English is <code>C* = &lt;circle ID, candidate boundary, membership eligibility, role rules, entry and exit, internal state, external interfaces, material channels, experience–meaning channels, constraints, time window, scale profile, identity criterion, evidential status, counterexamples&gt;</code>. <a href="#fnref3" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn4" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: The full tuple in English is <code>MC* = &lt;actor set A, circle set C, circle relations Rcc, membership and role mapping Rac, material state M, experience–meaning state Ψ, channels and constraints Q, event stream E, multiple clocks T, scale profile SP, evidential status W, identity criterion K&gt;</code>. <a href="#fnref4" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
